# AoU-LR data boundary

## Primary data stays in Terra

All of Us rules forbid downloading primary data: BAMs, CRAMs, VCF/BCFs
(integrated, GLnexus, SV, FLARE, TRGT) and their per-sample genotypes. They live
in Terra (workspace bucket `gs://fc-secure-8f7d6a20-04ce-40d7-8c88-aececeac3e09`,
the Phase 2 storage workspace, `gs://prod-drc-broad/...`). Never suggest
downloading or streaming them to the laptop, or running "quick checks" on them
locally. Test code on synthetic fixtures instead (`scripts/test_*.py`).

## Limited participant tables may be local

Covariates, sample lists, id orders and similar small tables (e.g.
`covariates.v8.csv.gz`, `tractor_mix/resources/`) are permitted locally for
practical work. They must never be committed (this repo is on GitHub) or shared
outside the AoU long-read working group. Keep them gitignored; never `git add`
them, and flag any that are untracked but not ignored.

## How work reaches the data

1. Scripts, notebooks and resources go to the workspace bucket: commit and push,
   then run `notebooks/terra/00_sync_repo.ipynb` on Terra, or the user uploads
   them by hand (gitignored resources always by hand).
2. WDLs are uploaded **manually** by the user to the Terra Methods Repository;
   `00_sync_repo` only stages copies under `$WORKSPACE_BUCKET/wdl/`.
3. Analyses run in Terra notebooks or workflows.

## What comes back

Derived outputs: summary TSVs and JSONs, text summaries, figures, metrics. Ones
meant for the manuscript repo carry no research_ids (small cells may be plotted).

When proposing work, say which steps the user runs on Terra and which outputs
to bring back, rather than offering to run them locally.
